{"id":"838d95e4c53f4d53f21296d0106e525b929429ac","axis":"silent","repo":"aws/aws-sdk-js-v3","sha":"838d95e4c53f4d53f21296d0106e525b929429ac","parsed":{"files":[{"path":"clients/client-kms/README.md","lines":["@@ -35,6 +35,19 @@ support</a> in the KMS Developer Guide.</p>"," support cipher suites with Perfect Forward Secrecy (PFS) such as Ephemeral Diffie-Hellman"," (DHE) or Elliptic Curve Ephemeral Diffie-Hellman (ECDHE). Most modern systems such as Java 7"," and later support these modes.</p>","+<note>","+<p>With current technology, brute force attacks on KMS TLS cipher suite key exchange","+mechanisms are not feasible. However, cipher suites that use Rivest–Shamir–Adleman (RSA)","+or Diffie-Hellman (DH) key exchange will be susceptible if large-scale quantum computing","+becomes practical.","+<a href=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ir/2024/NIST.IR.8547.ipd.pdf\">NIST IR 8547</a>","+on the NIST website establishes a timeline to phase out quantum-vulnerable algorithms","+by 2030 and disallow them entirely by 2035. If your applications rely on the long-term","+confidentiality of KMS API traffic, we recommend that you","+<a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/kms/latest/developerguide/pqtls.html\">use post-quantum TLS with KMS</a>.","+For more information about post-quantum cybersecurity, see <a href=\"https://www.nsa.gov/Cybersecurity/Post-Quantum-Cybersecurity-Resources/\">Post-Quantum Cybersecurity Resources</a> on the","+National Security Agency (NSA) website.</p>","+</note>"," <p>"," <b>Signing Requests</b>"," </p>","@@ -320,6 +333,13 @@ CreateKey"," </details>"," <details>"," <summary>","+Decapsulate","+</summary>","+","+[Command API Reference](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSJavaScriptSDK/v3/latest/client/kms/command/DecapsulateCommand/) / [Input](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSJavaScriptSDK/v3/latest/Package/-aws-sdk-client-kms/Interface/DecapsulateCommandInput/) / [Output](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSJavaScriptSDK/v3/latest/Package/-aws-sdk-client-kms/In